---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Roadmap" description="amber roadmap — what's coming next, within the mission">
  <NavBar />
  <main>
    <h1>Roadmap</h1>

    <section class="section">
      <p>
        amber is a bare-metal C++ agent harness for Linux. The roadmap is about
        expanding what the harness can do — not adding UI polish, not chasing
        platforms outside the mission. Linux is the armour; the roadmap stays
        inside it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider-thick" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Near Term</h2>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>Long-duration research — tmux integration, days and weeks of autonomous work</li>
        <li>Semantic search backends — real embedding models for code search</li>
        <li>Skills system — authored and learned skills, project-scoped memory</li>
        <li>Background job notifications in the status bar</li>
        <li>MCP ecosystem — more servers, richer tooling over the existing client</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Mid Term</h2>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>Multi-agent orchestration — sub-agents with shared context, parallel execution</li>
        <li>Compression pipeline visibility — live progress in the TUI</li>
        <li>Config key-chain — unlimited-depth configuration from the command line</li>
        <li>File-path completion — bash/zsh standard behaviour</li>
        <li>Memory and skill promotion UI — learn, inspect, pin, export</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Far Term</h2>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>Self-hosted LLM tooling — local inference, quantized models, VRAM management</li>
        <li>Distributed agent clusters — multiple agents on the same project</li>
        <li>Multi-window background jobs — the agent keeps working while you switch windows</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Not on the Roadmap</h2>
      <p>Some things amber is not going to do — by design, not by accident:</p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li><strong>Windows or macOS ports</strong> — Linux is the armour. Wine and Cygwin are not Linux.</li>
        <li><strong>Web or GUI frontends</strong> — the terminal is the API. SSH exists.</li>
        <li><strong>Plugin loaders</strong> — every UNIX tool is already a plugin. Skills and MCP cover the rest.</li>
        <li><strong>SaaS or telemetry</strong> — local-first. Your data stays on your machine.</li>
        <li><strong>Mobile apps</strong> — amber lives on the server.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
